I recently stumbled upon code like this:
-----------------
var x = parseInt(str, 10);
if (x == NaN) {
   alert('invalid input');
}
-------------
The programmer had obviously mistaken isNaN check for a comparison with NaN. Any comparison with NaN (even NaN == NaN) will result in false.

Though this might not be a very common mistake I still think the compiler could issue a warning in this case.
You already have the &quot;WARNING - condition always evaluates to false&quot; message for example.

Secondly, the compiler could optimize here, not even in advanced mode the code is short-circuited:
------
NaN==parseInt(&quot;asdf&quot;,10)&amp;&amp;alert(&quot;error&quot;);
------

Regards
/ Fredrik

comments.body:
Although the warning is now added as suggested, the compiler still doesn't short-circuit optimize.
I.e even in advanced mode the above example outputs: NaN==parseInt(str,10)&&alert("invalid input");

/Fredrik Blomqvist

