If the first parameter of a method on a class object is $super, Prototype's code for setting up the class (in 
addMethod)  inserts a reference to the superclass.  Here's the relevant code:

Class.Methods {
  addMethods: function(source) {
    var ancestor   = this.superclass && this.superclass.prototype;
    var properties = Object.keys(source);
   ...
   for (var i = 0, length = properties.length; i < length; i++) {
      var property = properties[i], value = source[property];
      if (ancestor && Object.isFunction(value) &&
          value.argumentNames().first() == "$super") {
         ....

Alan added a change in November to avoid renaming $super by marking it as an exported symbol in the 
DefaultCodingConvention.  To correctly compile Prototype code in advanced mode, we'll need to make sure 
the setup code for the class correctly does the parameter substitution.

See
[URL]
